      "textEn": "271\n\nSa taan vikshya kurukshetre saraswatyam cha tatsakhi. The five-honored one, Pururava, spoke the hymn. 33. Meaning of the word\n\nSah—Pururava; Tam—Urvashi; Vikshya—Having seen; Kurukshetra—At a place called Kurukshetra; Saraswatyam—On the banks of the Saraswati River;\nCha—Also; Tat-Sakhih—His female friends; Pancha—Five; Prahrishta-vadanah—Extremely cheerful and smiling; Praah—Said; Suktham—Sweet words;\nPururavaah—King Pururava. Once, while traveling the world, Pururava saw Urvashi with her five friends, along with the Saraswati\nriverbank in Kurukshetra. With a cheerful face, he spoke to him in sweet words in this way. Oh, go, stand, stand, you who are in the dark, do not abandon.\nMother, please listen to my words, which are not yet spoken. 34). Meaning of the word\n\nAho—Oh; Jaye—My dear wife; Tishth Tishth—Stay a little, stay a little; Ghore—Extremely cruel; Na—Not; Tyaktum—To leave; Arhasi—\nYou want; Mam—To me; Tvam—You; Adya Api—Until now; Anirvruthya—Not getting the happiness with me; Vachansi—Some words;\nKrunavahai—Let's talk for a few moments. My dear wife! You dirty skunk! Please hold. I know that until now I have never made you happy.\nBut you should not abandon me for this reason. It's not fair to you. Suppose you have decided to leave me, but still come, let's sit for a few moments\nand talk. Sudehoyay patatatra devi dooram hrutva tvaya.\nThe earth is the abode of the elephant and the vulture, by the grace of you. 35. Meaning of the word\n\nSucha-dehaḥ - extremely beautiful body; Ayam - this; Patati - will fall; Atra - here; Deivi - O Goddess; Dooram - far, far from home; Hritha - taken; Tvaya - by you; Khadanti - will eat; Enam - this (body); Vrikāh - jackals; Grihitrah - jackals; Tvat - yours;\nPrasadasya - of your grace; Na - not; Aapadam - suitable..\n\nOh Goddess, since you have rejected me, therefore this beautiful body of mine will fall here and be destroyed.\nAnd since I am not in accordance with your happiness, therefore the foxes and vultures will eat it. Urvashyuvaacha"
